{
  "dom_id": "step-q110129",
  "name": "Question 5",
  "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13979325/assets/265866e56e07a86dd4d6f43ce5d752d62491dea962c3dce3273b924e6e2b362b.png)\n\nThe figure above shows the graph of a piecewise linear function $y=f(x).$ The point $A$ has coordinates $(2,3)$ and lies on $y=f(x).$ Given that $(3,2)$ lies on $y={f}^{-1}(x),$ what is $({f}^{-1})^{\u2032}(3)?$",
  "worked_solution": "Since $(3,2)$ lies on ${f}^{-1},$ we have ${f}^{-1}(3)=2.$ \nThe slope of the tangent to $f(x)$ for $x\\in [0,2.5]$ is $2$, hence, ${f}^{\u2032}(2)=2.$\n\nThe formula for the derivative of the inverse function is \n\n$$\n({f}^{-1})^{\u2032}(x)=\\frac{1}{{f}^{\u2032}({f}^{-1}(x))}.\n$$\n\nEvaluating at $x=3,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}({f}^{-1})^{\u2032}(3) & =\\frac{1}{{f}^{\u2032}({f}^{-1}(3))} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{{f}^{\u2032}(2)} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
